<div class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span>The Friday Recap</div><h1>A Jobs Report That Cut the <em>Wrong Way</em></h1><p class="dek">For once the market wanted a weak number. Instead August payrolls came in three times as strong as forecast — and July's contraction was revised away. Good news for the economy, bad news for the rate-hike odds, and stocks fell.</p><div class="byline"><div class="who"><b>Hunter William Lang</b><span class="role">Markets — The Friday Recap</span></div><div class="when">Fri · Sep 4, 2026</div></div><div class="body"><p class="lead">It has been that kind of year — the kind where a strong economy is a problem for stocks. On Friday the government said U.S. employers added 162,000 jobs in August, roughly triple the 45,000-to-55,000 economists expected, and revised the prior two months higher, turning July's alarming contraction into a positive number. In a normal cycle that is unambiguously good news. In this one, with Warsh openly weighing a hike, it read as a green light to tighten — and the S&amp;P fell 0.4% to 7,718.60.</p><div class="scoreboard"><div class="cell"><div class="lbl">S&P 500</div><div class="val">7,718.60</div><div class="chg down">-0.38%</div></div><div class="cell"><div class="lbl">Nasdaq</div><div class="val">26,506.99</div><div class="chg down">-0.29%</div></div><div class="cell"><div class="lbl">Dow</div><div class="val">53,414.25</div><div class="chg down">-0.51%</div></div><div class="cell"><div class="lbl">2-yr Yield</div><div class="val">4.37%</div><div class="chg down">▲ hike bets</div></div></div><p>The unemployment rate held steady at 4.1%, as expected, and the upward revisions were the sting in the tail: the very July weakness that had let the market dream of a patient Fed was, it turns out, a mirage. Treasury yields jumped, with the policy-sensitive 2-year climbing to 4.37%, its highest since January 2025. Futures lifted the odds of a rate hike at the Fed's meeting later this month to roughly 58%, up from about 50% the day before.</p><h2>The week, day by day</h2><div class="ledger"><div class="row"><div class="day">Mon 8/31</div><div class="note">Renewed U.S.–Iran strikes and a hawkish Fed hangover sink the Dow 374 points.</div><div class="num">7,686.14<span class="d down">-0.3%</span></div></div><div class="row"><div class="day">Wed 9/2</div><div class="note">Tech snaps a three-day slide even as Brent settles near $96.</div><div class="num">7,666.60<span class="d up">+0.5%</span></div></div><div class="row"><div class="day">Thu 9/3</div><div class="note">A broad relief rally lifts the tape into the jobs report.</div><div class="num">7,747.71<span class="d up">+1.1%</span></div></div><div class="row"><div class="day">Fri 9/4</div><div class="note">Hot payrolls (+162K) and rising yields flip the mood — good news, sold.</div><div class="num">7,718.60<span class="d down">-0.4%</span></div></div></div><p>For the week the S&amp;P eked out a 0.1% gain and the Nasdaq rose 0.4%, salvaged by Thursday's rally, while the Dow slipped 0.3%. It was a volatile, headline-driven stretch bookended by a Middle East shock on Monday and a labor-market shock on Friday.</p><h2>The politics get loud</h2><p>The report also drew the White House into the frame. President Trump praised the jobs number and publicly pressed the Fed to lower rates, warning he would curb trade with certain countries if it did not — a pointed intervention as the central bank weighs the opposite move. The clash sets up a September meeting in which monetary independence itself is part of the story, not just the rate decision.</p><blockquote class="pull">A market rooting for a weak jobs report got a strong one. The economy won; the doves lost; and the Fed's path just got clearer — and harder.<span class="attrib">The Friday Recap</span></blockquote><div class="takeaway"><div class="t">The bottom line</div><p>A strong economy pushed stocks down because it pushed hike odds up — now near 58%, with the 2-year at 4.37%. July's contraction was revised away, gutting the dovish case. All eyes turn to the Fed's meeting later this month, with a president loudly demanding the opposite of what the data now supports.</p></div></div>  <div class="disclaimer">
